---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="About | Tejas Kabra" description="About Tejas Kabra, an electrical engineering student at Texas A&M.">
  <section class="shell py-20 md:py-28">
    <p class="eyebrow text-signal">About</p>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[0.95fr_1.05fr] lg:items-end">
      <div>
        <h1 class="text-6xl font-semibold leading-[0.92] tracking-[-0.065em] md:text-8xl">
          Tejas Kabra
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-xl text-2xl leading-9 text-ink/72">
          Electrical Engineering student at Texas A&amp;M building embedded hardware, PCB systems,
          telemetry, power electronics, and robotics projects.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-l border-t p-7 hairline md:p-9">
        <p class="technical-label text-signal">Summary</p>
        <p class="mt-5 text-xl leading-9 text-ink/78">
          I like engineering problems that are difficult for a real reason: the board has to power on,
          the firmware has to talk to the hardware, the signals have to survive the environment, and
          the system still has to be understandable when something breaks.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-8 text-ink/62">
          Long term, space is the goal that keeps me pointed forward. I want something I build to
          operate beyond Earth someday, and that pushes me to get better at hardware design, firmware,
          testing, and debugging with actual systems in front of me.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="scoreboard border-y hairline text-ink">
    <div class="shell grid md:grid-cols-3">
      {[
        ["Studying", "B.S. Electrical Engineering", "Texas A&M University"],
        ["Building", "Vehicle telemetry and embedded boards", "CAN, LTE, STM32, PCB bring-up"],
        ["Seeking", "Fall 2026 co-op", "Electrical, embedded, hardware, or systems"],
      ].map(([label, value, detail]) => (
        <div class="border-b border-ink/15 p-7 md:min-h-48 md:border-b-0 md:border-r md:last:border-r-0">
          <span class="technical-label text-signal">{label}</span>
          <strong class="score-digit mt-12 block text-2xl">{value}</strong>
          <span class="mt-2 block text-sm text-ink/45">{detail}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell grid gap-14 py-24 md:py-32 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr]">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow text-signal">Profile</p>
      <h2 class="mt-6 text-5xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.05em]">About me</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-9 text-ink/68">
      <p>
        I am an Electrical Engineering student at Texas A&amp;M and a Craig and Galen Brown Engineering
        Honors student. Most of my work sits at the boundary between PCB design, embedded firmware,
        power architecture, and system integration.
      </p>
      <p>
        The project that has shaped me most so far is the CAN-LTE telemetry system I designed with
        Texas A&amp;M Formula Electric. I designed a 4-layer STM32G4-based PCB with CAN, UART, power
        regulation, and a Quectel EG25 LTE modem, then carried it through two revisions, firmware
        development, hardware testing, and vehicle integration.
      </p>
      <p>
        That project taught me that a schematic working in theory is only the beginning. Power rails
        have to behave correctly, interfaces have to communicate reliably, and the whole system has
        to be organized well enough that the team can debug it under pressure.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have also worked on electrical design for modular data center cooling infrastructure at
        Southland Industries, an STM32H7 development board for Texas A&amp;M RoboMasters, a high-side
        NMOS transient suppression board, and a Raspberry Pi based automated inventory shelf for
        Formula Electric.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-panel/45 py-24 md:py-32">
    <div class="shell grid gap-14 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow text-signal">Engineering style</p>
        <h2 class="mt-6 text-5xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.05em]">How I work</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        {[
          ["Start with constraints", "Define the electrical, mechanical, timing, power, and interface requirements before choosing parts or routing boards."],
          ["Build complete systems", "Think through power, firmware, connectors, mechanical access, testing, and serviceability as one design problem."],
          ["Bring hardware up carefully", "Use DMMs, oscilloscopes, test points, logs, and small subsystem checks before trusting the full system."],
          ["Revise from evidence", "Use failures, measurements, and layout issues to make the next revision cleaner and easier to debug."],
        ].map(([title, body], index) => (
          <article class="min-h-56 border p-6 hairline">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-signal">0{index + 1}</span>
            <h3 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.035em]">{title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-ink/58">{body}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell py-24 md:py-32">
    <p class="eyebrow text-signal">Current direction</p>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_0.95fr] lg:items-start">
      <h2 class="text-5xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.055em] md:text-7xl">
        I want to become the kind of electrical engineer who can own the full path from idea to
        working hardware.
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-lg leading-8 text-ink/64">
        <p>
          Right now, that means getting better at schematic decisions, PCB layout, firmware bring-up,
          board-level testing, and the practical debugging that happens when the real system does not
          behave like the clean version in the design review.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am looking for co-op work where I can contribute to hardware that has to perform outside
          of a classroom environment, learn from experienced engineers, and keep building systems that
          are measurable, testable, and useful.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 pt-3">
          <a class="border border-signal px-5 py-3 technical-label text-signal transition-colors hover:bg-signal hover:text-panel" href="/#projects">
            View projects
          </a>
          <a class="border border-ink/20 px-5 py-3 technical-label transition-colors hover:border-ink" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/tejas-kabra-0869aa291">
            LinkedIn
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
